  "lem-hh-dihedral-root-recurrence-and-root-sign": {
   "citations": [
    {
     "fact": "F1",
     "source": "def-hh-coxeter-matrix-word-group-and-length",
     "source_section": "Definition",
     "quote": "Define $$W:=F(S)/N,$$ and write $s$ (as well as $sN$) for the image of $s\\in S$ in $W$ ([[def-quotient-group]], [[def-group]]).",
     "uses": [
      "3.1",
      "4.1",
      "6.1",
      "7.1"
     ]
    },
    {
     "fact": "F2",
     "source": "def-hh-geometric-coxeter-representation-and-roots",
     "source_section": "Definition",
     "quote": "**Fix** one primitive $2m(s,t)$-th root of unity $\\zeta_{st}\\in K$ for each unordered finite edge (a finite selection, since $S$ is finite) and put $$c_{st}:=c_{ts}:=\\zeta_{st}+\\zeta_{st}^{-1}\\in K\\quad (m(s,t)<\\infty),\\qquad c_{st}:=c_{ts}:=2\\quad (m(s,t)=\\infty).$$ **The representation on the simple-root basis.** Let $E$ be the $K$-vector space with basis $(\\alpha_s)_{s\\in S}$ ([[def-vector-space]], [[def-linear-basis]]).",
     "uses": [
      "1.1",
      "2.2",
      "3.1",
      "7.1"
     ]
    },
    {
     "fact": "F3",
     "source": "def-linear-basis",
     "source_section": "Definition",
     "quote": "A subset $B \\subseteq V$ is a **basis of $V$** when - **(B1)** $B$ is linearly independent ([[def-linear-independence]]), and - **(B2)** $B$ spans $V$, that is $\\operatorname{span}(B) = V$ ([[def-linear-combination-and-span]], which is where the words *spans* and *spanning set* are fixed; they are not redefined here).",
     "uses": [
      "1.1",
      "2.1"
     ]
    },
    {
     "fact": "F3",
     "source": "def-linear-map",
     "source_section": "Definition",
     "quote": "A function $T:V\\to W$ is **linear** when $$T(au+bv)=aT(u)+bT(v)$$ for all $a,b\\in F$ and $u,v\\in V$.",
     "uses": [
      "1.1",
      "2.1"
     ]
    },
    {
     "fact": "F4",
     "source": "def-internal-direct-sum",
     "source_section": "Definition",
     "quote": "**The definition.** $V$ is the **internal direct sum** of the family $U$, written $$V \\;=\\; \\bigoplus_{i<n} U_i ,$$ when both of the following hold: - **(D1)** $\\displaystyle\\sum_{i<n} U_i = V$; - **(D2)** for every $j < n$, $\\displaystyle U_j \\cap \\sum_{i \\ne j} U_i = \\{0_V\\}$.",
     "uses": [
      "2.1"
     ]
    },
    {
     "fact": "F5",
     "source": "def-characteristic-polynomial-of-an-operator",
     "source_section": "Definition",
     "quote": "Choose an ordered basis $\\mathcal B$ and define $$\\chi_T(x):=\\chi_{[T]_{\\mathcal B}^{\\mathcal B}}(x).$$ Matrices representing the same endomorphism in different ordered bases are similar, and similar matrices have the same characteristic polynomial.",
     "uses": [
      "2.3"
     ]
    },
    {
     "fact": "F5",
     "source": "def-characteristic-polynomial-of-a-matrix",
     "source_section": "Definition",
     "quote": "The **characteristic polynomial of $A$** is $$\\chi_A(x):=\\det(xI_n-A)\\in F[x].$$ For $n=0$, define the characteristic polynomial of the unique $0\\times0$ matrix to be the constant polynomial $1$.",
     "uses": [
      "2.3"
     ]
    },
    {
     "fact": "F5",
     "source": "lem-characteristic-polynomial-is-monic-and-has-extreme-coefficients",
     "source_section": "Statement",
     "quote": "For $A\\in M_n(F)$, the polynomial $\\chi_A(x)$ is monic of degree $n$.",
     "uses": [
      "2.3"
     ]
    },
    {
     "fact": "F6",
     "source": "def-eigenvalue-eigenvector-eigenspace-and-spectrum",
     "source_section": "Definition",
     "quote": "A scalar $\\lambda\\in F$ is an **eigenvalue** of $T$ when there is a nonzero vector $v\\in V$ such that $$T(v)=\\lambda v.$$ Such a vector is an **eigenvector belonging to $\\lambda$**.",
     "uses": [
      "2.3"
     ]
    },
    {
     "fact": "F6",
     "source": "cor-distinct-characteristic-roots-imply-diagonalisability",
     "source_section": "Statement",
     "quote": "If the characteristic polynomial of a finite-dimensional endomorphism splits over $F$ into distinct linear factors, then the endomorphism is diagonalisable over $F$.",
     "uses": [
      "2.3"
     ]
    },
    {
     "fact": "F7",
     "source": "def-order-in-a-group",
     "source_section": "Definition",
     "quote": "If $S_g \\ne \\varnothing$, the **order of $g$** is its least element, $$\\operatorname{ord}(g) \\;:=\\; \\min S_g \\;\\in\\; \\mathbb{N},$$ which exists by the well-ordering principle ([[thm-well-ordering-principle]]): every nonempty subset of $\\mathbb{N}$ has a least element, and that element is unique, being $\\le$ every element of $S_g$ and a member of it.",
     "uses": [
      "2.3",
      "3.1",
      "4.1"
     ]
    },
    {
     "fact": "F8",
     "source": "def-roots-of-unity-in-a-field",
     "source_section": "Definition",
     "quote": "An element $\\zeta\\in\\mu_n(K)\\subseteq K^{\\times}$ is a **primitive $n$-th root of unity** when its order in the group $K^{\\times}$ is exactly $n$ ([[def-order-in-a-group]]): $$\\operatorname{ord}(\\zeta)=n .$$ Equivalently, $\\zeta^n=1$ and no exponent $k$ with $1\\le k<n$ has $\\zeta^k=1$.",
     "uses": [
      "2.3"
     ]
    }
   ],
   "derivations": [
    {
     "id": "1.1",
     "claim": "**The rank-two block.** Since $\\sigma_t(\\alpha_s)=\\alpha_s+c\\,\\alpha_t$ and $\\sigma_t(\\alpha_t)=-\\alpha_t$ with $c=c_{st}$, linearity of $\\sigma_s$ gives $A(\\alpha_s)=\\sigma_s(\\alpha_s+c\\,\\alpha_t)=(c^2-1)\\alpha_s+c\\,\\alpha_t$ and $A(\\alpha_t)=\\sigma_s(-\\alpha_t)=-c\\,\\alpha_s-\\alpha_t$, so $A(P)\\subseteq P$ and the matrix of $B=A|_P$ in the basis $(\\alpha_s,\\alpha_t)$ is $\\begin{pmatrix}c^2-1&-c\\\\ c&-1\\end{pmatrix}$, whose trace is $(c^2-1)+(-1)=c^2-2$ and whose determinant is $(c^2-1)(-1)-(-c)c=1$. For $u\\notin\\{s,t\\}$ one has $A(\\alpha_u)=\\sigma_s(\\alpha_u+c_{tu}\\alpha_t)=\\alpha_u+(c_{su}+c_{tu}c)\\alpha_s+c_{tu}\\alpha_t$, so $A(\\alpha_u)-\\alpha_u\\in P$; by linearity $A(v)-v\\in P$ for every $v\\in E$.",
     "step": "1.1",
     "inputs": [
      "F2",
      "F3"
     ]
    },
    {
     "id": "2.1",
     "claim": "**The two-block recursion.** The subspace $Q=\\operatorname{span}\\{\\alpha_u:u\\notin\\{s,t\\}\\}$ satisfies $P\\cap Q=\\{0\\}$ and $P+Q=E$ because the basis of $E$ splits into the basis $(\\alpha_s,\\alpha_t)$ of $P$ and the basis of $Q$; hence $E=P\\oplus Q$, and every $v\\in E$ has unique coordinates $v=p+q$ with $p\\in P$, $q\\in Q$. I claim that for every $k\\ge1$ and all such $p,q$ one has $A^k(p+q)=B^k p+S_k\\bigl(A(q)-q\\bigr)+q$, where $S_k:=\\mathrm{id}_P+B+\\cdots+B^{k-1}$. For $k=1$ this reads $A(p)+A(q)=Bp+(A(q)-q)+q$, which holds because $A|_P=B$. If it holds for $k$, then $B^kp+S_k(A(q)-q)\\in P$ and $A(q)-q\\in P$ by step 1.1, so applying $A$ and using $A|_P=B$ gives $A^{k+1}(p+q)=B\\bigl(B^kp+S_k(A(q)-q)\\bigr)+A(q)=B^{k+1}p+\\bigl(BS_k+\\mathrm{id}_P\\bigr)\\bigl(A(q)-q\\bigr)+q$, and $BS_k+\\mathrm{id}_P=B+B^2+\\cdots+B^k+\\mathrm{id}_P=S_{k+1}$, which completes the induction.",
     "step": "2.1",
     "inputs": [
      "1.1",
      "F3",
      "F4"
     ]
    },
    {
     "id": "2.2",
     "claim": "**The cases $m=2$ and $m=\\infty$.** If $m=2$, then $\\zeta$ has order $4$, so $\\zeta^2=-1$ and $\\zeta^{-1}=-\\zeta$, whence $c=\\zeta+\\zeta^{-1}=0$; substituting into the matrix of step 1.1 leaves $B=\\begin{pmatrix}-1&0\\\\ 0&-1\\end{pmatrix}=-\\mathrm{id}_P$, so $B^2=\\mathrm{id}_P$ and $\\mathrm{id}_P+B=0$. If $m=\\infty$, then $c=2$ by the convention in [F2], and the matrix of step 1.1 gives $B=\\mathrm{id}_P+M$ with $M=B-\\mathrm{id}_P=\\begin{pmatrix}2&-2\\\\ 2&-2\\end{pmatrix}\\ne0$ and $M^2=0$. The binomial expansion in the commutative algebra of endomorphisms then gives $B^k=(\\mathrm{id}_P+M)^k=\\mathrm{id}_P+kM$ for every $k\\ge1$, and $kM\\ne0$ because $M\\ne0$ and $k\\cdot1_K\\ne0$ in the characteristic-zero field $K$; hence $B^k\\ne\\mathrm{id}_P$ for all $k\\ge1$, and in particular $B\\ne\\mathrm{id}_P$.",
     "step": "2.2",
     "inputs": [
      "1.1",
      "F2"
     ]
    },
    {
     "id": "2.3",
     "claim": "**The finite case $2<m<\\infty$.** By step 1.1 and [F5] the characteristic polynomial of $B$ is $\\chi_B(x)=x^2-(c^2-2)x+1$; since $c=\\zeta+\\zeta^{-1}$ satisfies $c^2-2=\\zeta^2+\\zeta^{-2}$ and $\\zeta^2\\zeta^{-2}=1$, one has $\\chi_B(x)=x^2-(\\zeta^2+\\zeta^{-2})x+1=(x-\\zeta^2)(x-\\zeta^{-2})$. The two roots are distinct: $\\zeta^2=\\zeta^{-2}$ would give $\\zeta^4=1$, impossible because $\\zeta$ has order $2m>4$. Hence the characteristic polynomial splits into distinct linear factors, and [F6] makes $B$ diagonalisable, with eigenvalues $\\zeta^2$ and $\\zeta^{-2}$. Consequently $B^m=\\mathrm{id}_P$, because on an eigenvector for $\\lambda\\in\\{\\zeta^2,\\zeta^{-2}\\}$ the operator $B^m$ scales it by $\\lambda^m=\\zeta^{\\pm2m}=1$ by [F7] and [F8]; the operator $S_m=\\mathrm{id}_P+B+\\cdots+B^{m-1}$ is zero, because the polynomial $1+x+\\cdots+x^{m-1}$ vanishes at both eigenvalues, where $\\zeta^{\\pm2m}=1$ and $\\zeta^{\\pm2}\\ne1$ since $0<2<2m$; and $B^k\\ne\\mathrm{id}_P$ for $0<k<m$, because on the eigenvector for $\\zeta^2$ it scales by $\\zeta^{2k}\\ne1$, again since $0<2k<2m$ and $2m$ is the least positive exponent killing $\\zeta$.",
     "step": "2.3",
     "inputs": [
      "1.1",
      "F5",
      "F6",
      "F7",
      "F8"
     ]
    },
    {
     "id": "3.1",
     "claim": "**Order of $A$, the relators, and the exact dihedral orders.** For finite $m$, step 2.1 gives $A^m(p+q)=B^mp+S_m(A(q)-q)+q=p+q$ for all $p,q$, using $B^m=\\mathrm{id}_P$ and $S_m=0$ from steps 2.2 and 2.3; hence $A^m=\\mathrm{id}_E$. If $0<k<m$, choose $p\\in P$ with $B^kp\\ne p$, which exists because $B^k\\ne\\mathrm{id}_P$; then $A^k(p)=B^kp\\ne p$, so $A^k\\ne\\mathrm{id}_E$ and $A$ has order exactly $m$. For $m=\\infty$, $B^k\\ne\\mathrm{id}_P$ for every $k\\ge1$ gives $A^k\\ne\\mathrm{id}_E$ for every $k\\ge1$, so $A$ has infinite order. Together with $\\sigma_s^2=\\mathrm{id}_E$, this shows that the map $f:S\\to\\mathrm{GL}_K(E)$, $f(s)=\\sigma_s$, satisfies the hypotheses of the universal property in [F1], so there is a unique homomorphism $\\sigma:W\\to\\mathrm{GL}_K(E)$ with $\\sigma(s)=\\sigma_s$ for all $s$. If $s\\ne t$ then $\\sigma_s\\ne\\sigma_t$, because $\\sigma_s(\\alpha_s)=-\\alpha_s$ while $\\sigma_t(\\alpha_s)=\\alpha_s+c_{st}\\alpha_t$ and $-2\\alpha_s-c_{st}\\alpha_t\\ne0$ by linear independence of $\\alpha_s,\\alpha_t$ and $2\\ne0$ in $K$; hence $s\\ne t$ in $W$. Finally, $st$ has order exactly $m$ when $m<\\infty$: writing $n$ for the order of $st$ in $W$, one has $n\\le m$ because $(st)^m=1$ in $W$ and $n$ is least, and $m\\le n$ because $A^n=\\sigma((st)^n)=\\mathrm{id}_E$ and $m$ is the least positive exponent killing $A$; hence $n=m$. When $m=\\infty$, a positive exponent $k$ with $(st)^k=1$ would give $A^k=\\mathrm{id}_E$, so no such exponent exists and $st$ has infinite order. Also $\\ell(s)=1$: $\\sigma_s\\ne\\mathrm{id}_E$ because $\\sigma_s(\\alpha_s)=-\\alpha_s\\ne\\alpha_s$, so $s\\ne1$ and $\\ell(s)\\ge1$ by [F1], while $s=s$ is a word of length $1$, so $\\ell(s)\\le1$.",
     "step": "3.1",
     "inputs": [
      "2.1",
      "2.2",
      "2.3",
      "F1",
      "F2",
      "F7"
     ]
    },
    {
     "id": "4.1",
     "claim": "**The signed action.** First, $U_s$ is a bijection of $\\{\\pm1\\}\\times T$ with $U_s^2=\\mathrm{id}$: for $r=s$ one has $U_s(\\varepsilon,s)=(-\\varepsilon,s)$ and applying $U_s$ again gives $(+\\varepsilon,s)$, while for $r\\ne s$ one has $srs\\ne s$ — if $srs=s$ then multiplying on the left by $s$ gives $rs=1$ and hence $r=s^{-1}=s$ using $s^2=1$ in $W$ — so $\\delta(s,srs)=0$ and the two applications of $U_s$ return $(\\varepsilon,r)$ from $U_s(\\varepsilon,srs)=(\\varepsilon,r)$. Next, fix a word $(s_1,\\dots,s_k)$ in $S$ with $w_j:=s_1\\cdots s_j$ and prefix reflections $r_i:=w_{i-1}s_iw_{i-1}^{-1}$; applying $U_{s_1},U_{s_2},\\dots,U_{s_k}$ successively to $(\\varepsilon,r)$ returns $\\bigl(\\varepsilon(-1)^{n(r)},\\,w_k^{-1}rw_k\\bigr)$ with $n(r):=\\#\\{i:r_i=r\\}$, by induction on $k$: the $i$-th step multiplies the sign by $(-1)^{\\delta(s_i,\\,w_{i-1}^{-1}rw_{i-1})}$, whose exponent is $1$ exactly when $r=w_{i-1}^{-1}s_iw_{i-1}=r_i$, and it conjugates the coordinate to $s_iw_{i-1}^{-1}rw_{i-1}s_i=(w_{i-1}s_i)^{-1}r(w_{i-1}s_i)$. Now let $a\\in\\{s,t\\}$ and let $b$ be the other of the two, and let $m=m(s,t)=m(t,s)<\\infty$; consider the alternating word $(a,b,a,b,\\dots)$ of length $2m$ and write $r_i=(ab)^{i-1}a$ for $1\\le i\\le 2m$ for its prefix reflections. The closed form is proved by induction on $i$: the conjugating prefix is $w_{i-1}=(ab)^{(i-1)/2}$ for odd $i$ and $w_{i-1}=(ab)^{(i-2)/2}a$ for even $i$, and the identities $(ab)^{-j}=(ba)^j$ and $a(ba)^j=(ab)^ja$ rewrite $w_{i-1}s_iw_{i-1}^{-1}$ as $(ab)^{i-1}a$ in both cases. Hence $r_{i+m}=(ab)^{i-1}(ab)^ma=(ab)^{i-1}a=r_i$ for $1\\le i\\le m$, while $r_1,\\dots,r_m$ are pairwise distinct: an equality $r_i=r_j$ with $i<j\\le m$ gives $(ab)^{j-i}=1$ after right multiplication by $a$, contradicting $0<j-i<m$ and the fact from step 3.1 that $ab$ has order exactly $m$. Therefore the sequence of $2m$ prefix reflections is $r_1,\\dots,r_m,r_1,\\dots,r_m$, so $n(r)\\in\\{0,2\\}$ for every $r\\in T$ and every accumulated sign $(-1)^{n(r)}$ is $1$; the conjugating coordinate returns to $r$ because $w_{2m}=(ab)^m=1$. So the permutation effected by each alternating word of length $2m$ is the identity, which for the two orders of the pair $\\{s,t\\}$ is exactly $U_s^2=\\mathrm{id}$ and $(U_sU_t)^m=\\mathrm{id}$ and $(U_tU_s)^m=\\mathrm{id}$. By [F1]'s universal property applied to $f:S\\to\\operatorname{Sym}(\\{\\pm1\\}\\times T)$, $f(s)=U_s$, there is then a homomorphism $\\rho:W\\to\\operatorname{Sym}(\\{\\pm1\\}\\times T)$ with $\\rho(s)=U_s$ for all $s$. Define $(\\varepsilon,r)\\cdot w:=\\rho(w^{-1})(\\varepsilon,r)$; this is a right action, because $(wv)^{-1}=v^{-1}w^{-1}$ gives $(\\varepsilon,r)\\cdot(wv)=\\rho(v^{-1})\\bigl(\\rho(w^{-1})(\\varepsilon,r)\\bigr)=((\\varepsilon,r)\\cdot w)\\cdot v$, and $(\\varepsilon,r)\\cdot s=\\rho(s)^{-1}(\\varepsilon,r)=U_s(\\varepsilon,r)$.",
     "step": "4.1",
     "inputs": [
      "3.1",
      "F1",
      "F7"
     ]
    },
    {
     "id": "5.1",
     "claim": "**Deletion.** Suppose $r_i=r_j$ for some $i<j$, that is $w_{i-1}s_iw_{i-1}^{-1}=w_{j-1}s_jw_{j-1}^{-1}$. Multiplying on the left by $w_{i-1}^{-1}$ and on the right by $w_{j-1}$ and using $w_{i-1}^{-1}w_{j-1}=s_i s_{i+1}\\cdots s_{j-1}$ gives $s_i\\,s_i s_{i+1}\\cdots s_{j-1}=s_i s_{i+1}\\cdots s_{j-1}s_j$, hence $s_i s_{i+1}\\cdots s_j=s_{i+1}\\cdots s_{j-1}$. Substituting this into the word $(s_1,\\dots,s_k)$ shows that deleting the two letters $s_i$ and $s_j$ leaves the value unchanged: $s_1\\cdots\\widehat{s_i}\\cdots\\widehat{s_j}\\cdots s_k=w$.",
     "step": "5.1",
     "inputs": [
      "4.1"
     ]
    },
    {
     "id": "6.1",
     "claim": "**Expression independence and reduced words.** For a word of $w$ the sign accumulated in step 4.1 is $(-1)^{n(r)}$, and the right action $(\\varepsilon,r)\\cdot w$ is independent of the word; comparing with the displayed formula for two words of $w$ shows that $(-1)^{n(r)}=\\eta(r,w)$ depends only on $w$ and $r$, and that $(\\varepsilon,r)\\cdot w=(\\varepsilon\\,\\eta(r,w),\\,w^{-1}rw)$ for every word of $w$. If the word $(s_1,\\dots,s_k)$ is reduced, then $n(r)\\le1$ for every $r$: if $n(r)\\ge2$, two indices would have $r_i=r_j$ with $i<j$, and step 5.1 would produce a word of length $k-2$ for $w$, contradicting $k=\\ell(w)$. Consequently the map $i\\mapsto r_i$ is injective, so the set $\\{r_1,\\dots,r_k\\}=\\{r\\in T:\\eta(r,w)=-1\\}$ has exactly $k=\\ell(w)$ elements; by the independence of $\\eta$ from the word, this set is independent of the reduced expression chosen.",
     "step": "6.1",
     "inputs": [
      "4.1",
      "5.1",
      "F1"
     ]
    },
    {
     "id": "7.1",
